`Iterable<T>` is defined in `java.lang` and has exactly one abstract method:

```java
Iterator<T> iterator();
```

When you write a for-each loop, the compiler translates it into an Iterator loop:

```java
// You write:
for (String s : list) { ... }
// Compiler sees:
Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) { String s = it.next(); ... }
```

Placing Iterable at the top means any class — not just collections — can be looped over. For example, a custom `FileLineReader` or `DatabaseResultSet` class can implement Iterable to enable for-each without being a List or Set.
